#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c70724-aaae-5f8b-90ba-0c075fe647ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321579} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3836ff-c967-550a-b987-849d6e75299d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321892} {:answer/id #uuid "62b7f57c-9049-5269-a2d4-b7a2f5cbdbc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321917} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b18d1-1602-5fab-90d5-83a3fb2ed5f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322404} {:answer/id #uuid "cdecf84e-1100-5475-ac8c-0fad8bf69a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62b7f57c-9049-5269-a2d4-b7a2f5cbdbc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321917} :answer-field/id #uuid "8225f4bf-8597-5194-8000-7f67e378df28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323430}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aec682f0-94b9-5c3a-a680-f47e16fecc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-36339" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6xy=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(10x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(10-6(1))\\,i+(2(1)-6)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,1)=⟨4,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{4} & =\\frac{y-1}{-4} \\\\ x-1 & =-y+1 \\\\ x+y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7e42f80-fb7f-5b14-bafa-151d9661671a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + 5y - 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123741} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbd747b-c83c-5643-8fc1-99aebcf0ce8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x - 3y + 5 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123742} {:answer/id #uuid "299fbc9a-5132-5d78-9c97-8f32aa21e331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x - 4y + 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123743} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb61ba3-eaf6-5a0b-aaa2-16d77d246f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x - 5y + 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123744} {:answer/id #uuid "76a8435c-542c-5a71-bac9-045158819127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x - 2y + 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123745}] :answer-field/id #uuid "22b9919e-c058-55c4-a73e-14ca053dbdce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123740}] :question/id #uuid "cb219d72-040d-55dd-90ca-3f831acb4d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-65173" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy = x^{2} + y^{2} - 7$. Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076ccae2-d102-5ad8-b1c2-ac28978e3e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263068} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3882de-2108-52bf-b5e1-4c3304b9a5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263257} {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a0770-0459-500c-aa32-352ae1667e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264813} {:answer/id #uuid "d493ac59-d9ae-5896-bd9e-006d111d56b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7e101e-fe81-5db4-a578-c892744685d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265605}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c80428ab-daca-506e-aca7-d4e40b0d604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130718" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9853cb-1b4d-5d99-b740-f207d47a13c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263384} {:answer/id #uuid "62cb3b0d-c5e0-5140-9b14-0b73eecbc9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263854} {:answer/id #uuid "70e32100-046e-5e0e-b9f2-eac7fe211b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263976} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d5900d-9aee-5b56-aa97-4e95d9c8f10a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} :answer-field/id #uuid "263638a6-9832-58e0-a5f7-da0d13d29e2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265316}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d42d4bf9-4eef-57b6-b4ed-d5448bfe2b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130723" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4xy+2{x}^{2}=x+2y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064b3549-4bf4-5a5c-b8dc-3968806ac3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263057} {:answer/id #uuid "1572d019-302d-55d4-a933-653804f7c50e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} {:answer/id #uuid "3a49bd46-cd0b-56cc-ad48-5f7f88b79995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263521} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab79447-463d-53f5-9910-346ef01f273b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} {:answer/id #uuid "d106df35-59e0-5715-a37e-1c3233721325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1572d019-302d-55d4-a933-653804f7c50e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca1f3923-ed50-5a12-bc40-f829c5055015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265604}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a95a9cd4-1a5a-56cf-b216-e3ff43ec1b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-130724" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2y-x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e39d31e-75b0-5793-8278-bebced97730a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x-7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321509} {:answer/id #uuid "372699ce-ec89-581d-9797-1841d9f9de0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321567} {:answer/id #uuid "8825796b-037c-5e7f-8b8b-68d0db08f793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+28=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322231} {:answer/id #uuid "90a9aaa5-ef19-541c-89c7-ebbd8c1de0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x+7y-139=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322294} {:answer/id #uuid "f052fdc2-234a-5694-9837-38ef76630d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "372699ce-ec89-581d-9797-1841d9f9de0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321567} :answer-field/id #uuid "a144aff6-eb9c-59ae-8c19-7bfb3a8dd26b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323481}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66ea4a99-e9fe-52c8-93b3-5bc45407e7da" :question/math-academy-id "q-36347" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2xy+6{y}^{2}=x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(8,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+6{y}^{2} & =x-2 \\\\ 2xy+6{y}^{2}-x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy+6{y}^{2}-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(8,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(8,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(8,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-1)\\,i+(2x+12y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(8,-3) & =(2(-3)-1)\\,i+(2(8)+12(-3))\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\,i-20\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-7,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(8,-3)=⟨-7,-20⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(8,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-8}{-7} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-20} \\\\ -20(x-8) & =-7(y+3) \\\\ -20x+7y+160+21 & =0 \\\\ -20x+7y+181 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c88cb4-d780-590b-b992-1f5a2d099b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336940} {:answer/id #uuid "b17a59bd-34cb-524b-a586-d668f0ab265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337621} {:answer/id #uuid "b93bd652-ee1e-5354-8880-3331e0abdb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337674} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5d67c-091f-5e66-b35b-d7bf5ddf3a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337771} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd19af6-18df-5dbc-8ca0-9ef0b733476e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcd19af6-18df-5dbc-8ca0-9ef0b733476e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338255} :answer-field/id #uuid "947eebdf-2bd7-5433-b16d-dbcb38a79114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338552}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05c970aa-8c53-5d2c-be67-cb9e3da21e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-71385" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =2(2)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨4,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)0-(y-0)4 & =0 \\\\ -4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfa2d9c-c5a6-5bae-b0d2-a5aba5899793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336165} {:answer/id #uuid "521e5638-e80d-5edb-bd60-91e3b7a98567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336787} {:answer/id #uuid "9255b74e-b684-545c-a101-b50d053e917e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337362} {:answer/id #uuid "928ea3e2-97a0-57df-ae69-6fb23ba7d7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337365} {:answer/id #uuid "c360c2bb-962a-5e23-b0d5-b54534c74210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9255b74e-b684-545c-a101-b50d053e917e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337362} :answer-field/id #uuid "59dddcfe-8a5c-5160-b7d7-f42966cf2e3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2de4f7e4-2884-5220-a39a-17359a9ea964" :question/math-academy-id "q-71388" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x+y-1=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241c7529-7f80-5365-a0c6-f7e836aaee88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336372} {:answer/id #uuid "391f7649-2e76-52fa-81b6-233716b6923c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336569} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb41a0a-f9a7-5281-9885-444e698af19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-x+8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336998} {:answer/id #uuid "b3143694-8d1c-519f-bf94-4aa69b2c3b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+2x+11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337629} {:answer/id #uuid "f204000c-6f32-5823-8c04-611b3011d48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3x+20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241c7529-7f80-5365-a0c6-f7e836aaee88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336372} :answer-field/id #uuid "4287e815-efb6-5dc2-9403-e74ef29f8446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338397}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1df9544-1831-5471-b207-bb07d395022e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71390" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}=4xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =4xy \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x-4y)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-3) & =(6(-1)-4(-3))\\,i+(2(-3)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,-3)=⟨6,-2⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-2} \\\\ (-2)(x+1) & =6(y+3) \\\\ -2x-2 & =6y+18 \\\\ 6y+2x+20 & =0 \\\\ 3y+x+10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923db4e3-1672-5a50-9581-8d103a4e9394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337357} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ca54cd-1662-5f06-ad4a-139620f93cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337484} {:answer/id #uuid "a201c603-2105-5c16-ac28-6ea1909ee0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500} {:answer/id #uuid "f0aa3752-46b2-55e2-872a-d4d7eb4de111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338139} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b5b220-5b4e-5b43-8acc-b11f2fdd7f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a201c603-2105-5c16-ac28-6ea1909ee0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500} :answer-field/id #uuid "a093c235-9586-5c2c-a6e1-6f61aa48334d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338573}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47afea57-2ac6-5bfd-8472-520b5199e74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71393" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)=2xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d40bef-5cc1-533e-a776-8178968f8226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373127} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5e25f1-f9d6-5abd-be1b-a979b00ae356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373128} {:answer/id #uuid "f370372e-718e-527d-b206-115386ee2633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373129} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3b4fbb-9302-5aaa-8ef1-476b737ddbbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373130} {:answer/id #uuid "a9003cf3-3b04-543a-a41c-c583ef479eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f370372e-718e-527d-b206-115386ee2633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c09f18f3-dc2a-5860-86fe-1086c3a20b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03866640-0a40-5a70-8301-d2aa021aa524" :question/math-academy-id "q-71394" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,4).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,4).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0)\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}(4)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,4)=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-0)\\frac{1}{2}-(y-4)0 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc8cbbb3-0607-5384-b0f5-a7494df17199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385517} {:answer/id #uuid "5f547d94-e922-5fd4-abac-19d655c1fdb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385518} {:answer/id #uuid "89704038-a5bc-5557-9fe3-df6fbe0297f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385519} {:answer/id #uuid "cd40c970-36db-556d-9137-5a131afc6b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385520} {:answer/id #uuid "459e2e49-2c9a-525a-a910-70de65a071a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd40c970-36db-556d-9137-5a131afc6b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385520} :answer-field/id #uuid "845fb0f9-d411-5b77-8fc8-10943f6e52ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50913094-4471-56fc-97f7-275492373a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-71386" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,0) & =\\frac{2}{9}(3)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,0)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)0-(y-0)\\frac{2}{3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5decc890-1d01-5ef2-9154-9ed30e0d6582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385524} {:answer/id #uuid "a0817702-700e-5e7c-8da2-a35776ec98ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385525} {:answer/id #uuid "569c2b8b-c5da-52e2-b41d-09fbec22be67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385526} {:answer/id #uuid "0109f1c6-5278-5400-a02b-474b0e4272e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385527} {:answer/id #uuid "92ed1ce6-3824-5dec-a381-53a6e97e1d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5decc890-1d01-5ef2-9154-9ed30e0d6582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385524} :answer-field/id #uuid "f683d60b-f987-566f-8e73-598501a40b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385523}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a6c1cae-376e-5dfa-b07c-5886b3b91c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71389" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2x)\\,i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-2\\cdot (-1))\\,i+(-(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,0)=⟨2,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{2} & =-y \\\\ x+1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1d5e01-e81c-5544-a929-055747dc7f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406774} {:answer/id #uuid "82778c6a-7758-50ec-ad08-3720a9d490ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406777} {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc8cce-7237-5eb6-ac41-6a5901ea8c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406780} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf6508f-66f3-52c3-8fbf-477ac88383db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e7ba4e-7d61-5628-8ce5-894dfba410f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406785}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef115c0b-3981-5c8f-8e9d-f41c20799c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130719" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2y-x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2y}+1)i+(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}})-2)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{y}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{1}-2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x-2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(y-1) \\\\ -6(x-2) & =3(y-1) \\\\ -2(x-2) & =y-1 \\\\ -2x+4 & =y-1 \\\\ 2x+y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364ddf2b-cdd1-5e21-88c2-478a7d637e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423045} {:answer/id #uuid "415a3975-289f-5c0a-aa48-bc85b813f82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423046} {:answer/id #uuid "6321cb0e-8e68-5a7b-9a36-ac871cc17b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423047} {:answer/id #uuid "82ce7cc2-be21-5dc4-9b04-e941ddd03d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423048} {:answer/id #uuid "ba63a292-9d83-540d-ac68-00ce4812ebb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ce7cc2-be21-5dc4-9b04-e941ddd03d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423048} :answer-field/id #uuid "02401848-522e-501b-82ff-6e5cb3f48ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423050}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f16c0b1-b80b-5067-adaf-a65b04fb7bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130721" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${e}^{{x}^{2}y}={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}y} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9 \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}+3{x}^{2})i+({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-2y)j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =(2(2)(0){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}+3({2}^{2}))i+(({2}^{2}){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}-2(0))j \\\\  & =12\\,i+4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨12,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨12,4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{12} & =\\frac{y-0}{4} \\\\ 4(x-2) & =12y \\\\ x-2 & =3y \\\\ x-3y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbad3809-ba80-59f9-ba4c-d639a5fee9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i - 2j + 4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123069} {:answer/id #uuid "0bfccc86-e9f7-5243-8a84-7790a342f294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4j + k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123070} {:answer/id #uuid "47a26aab-bf98-583d-a150-8a48058ef8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4i - 2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123071} {:answer/id #uuid "765db6cc-9dc8-511b-be71-d07c04b80a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i + 2j + 4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123072} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdd6503-ab90-5063-bf80-5d64dfc58891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i + j + 4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123073}] :answer-field/id #uuid "52fb938b-ddb8-55c8-9990-18252f0b9f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284eed2b-f5b5-55e8-8043-dc7f9352df8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385391} {:answer/id #uuid "891daeba-0e3b-599d-ba1c-f3ca19efea95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385392} {:answer/id #uuid "7479fa2d-d3ba-5e5f-b91f-c455aeddd593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385393} {:answer/id #uuid "f368beea-18d3-5ba4-a48e-57ffd46d193b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385394} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb18261-78e6-5c3a-b541-e09f14d04051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7479fa2d-d3ba-5e5f-b91f-c455aeddd593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385393} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6793d18-67c7-5552-bd0c-a7ad3c9a1562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385390}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5878a311-1e95-57bd-8bd7-391f384f5bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37570" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+5x+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(y+5)\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(-1+5)\\,i+(1)\\,j+2(2)\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,i+j+4\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68112ca-e7ee-5f12-8c6b-6f964b2fce64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i+4j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398915} {:answer/id #uuid "5739b0cd-fba7-5818-952f-616d15f8cd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-4j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398916} {:answer/id #uuid "fc61dd7b-3fa9-5347-ad13-eae563990528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398917} {:answer/id #uuid "9e270cd2-2ed5-5ff9-a8d2-046b4683fe91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,i+4j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398918} {:answer/id #uuid "12f59294-d897-5332-9fd4-6a0a58840be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2j+6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5739b0cd-fba7-5818-952f-616d15f8cd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-4j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398916} :answer-field/id #uuid "027e0dd0-8fb0-53bb-95b9-27c1e0e20a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398914}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15fe86f3-62bf-5e0d-8567-00ad11df9f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65167" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-2yz+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(2xy-2z)\\text{ }j+(-2y+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+(2(1)(-1)-2(1))\\,j+(-2(-1)+1)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i-4j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\,i-4\\,j+3\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050f1921-3c44-5bbf-b7ea-35eab2c32786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263045} {:answer/id #uuid "6abc818b-9a7d-590c-806b-551c7bbe6fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263916} {:answer/id #uuid "a6997c32-93ac-521d-9b24-002782474a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264426} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0b44e3-00a5-5e34-b4f1-766b6565f684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264496} {:answer/id #uuid "e957c96d-da16-5583-8c41-1dbbf28e3ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abc818b-9a7d-590c-806b-551c7bbe6fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263916} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a084402-00ad-5a44-a106-2a26851c80b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265454}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d357ddc-9cee-5e05-b7e3-0ba7beb5d8f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131115" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3784325a-a369-50b5-91bd-19b0506f0065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333499} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f25158-cf49-5e7b-a678-2a7edff63f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334412} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b97395-2591-5fe7-a765-24e75cd37f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334425} {:answer/id #uuid "e42e7344-8685-5612-9437-27f80a5f22bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334977} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b5911-a43a-5716-a002-b2a98cb3a797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3784325a-a369-50b5-91bd-19b0506f0065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333499} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d7321b-1e15-5d1e-9dec-8cc8fec04036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335524}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f338c4bb-049a-5229-858d-81a11a4613ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-65168" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17754529-babb-5cf0-8d95-1b9cc9537939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336252} {:answer/id #uuid "2027b715-6614-5f66-82b2-1f71af6cb775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+\\frac{1}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336332} {:answer/id #uuid "36a67130-4f88-5f7d-9e66-e328e09a4789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336538} {:answer/id #uuid "530705a9-532d-591f-bbf7-492c24eb6728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336798} {:answer/id #uuid "8a797477-b113-50ff-8e91-509061d9a654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a797477-b113-50ff-8e91-509061d9a654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280} :answer-field/id #uuid "380e5863-1f99-5c29-acf5-cfab37a637d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7aa18077-2806-56e2-99dd-f7365f248356" :question/math-academy-id "q-71403" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(3,4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(3,4,0) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217b6b69-1258-52ca-92cb-84d166055cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336344} {:answer/id #uuid "8847c9cc-20d2-574c-a780-1b2f9a38d565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337260} {:answer/id #uuid "9b429560-704b-5928-a704-fbc5ba05c495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337439} {:answer/id #uuid "edd7d436-4244-565c-b127-7c866996e6d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338108} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dcdca1-0652-5268-9a5c-8bdbc0a1e227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8847c9cc-20d2-574c-a780-1b2f9a38d565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337260} :answer-field/id #uuid "b348b668-7c03-56ec-b406-92e416034172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338605}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7acbac43-bc81-5177-9def-8ee007ede43b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71404" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421dd9cb-d37a-51b2-bf54-7fecaeaa111e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+e\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371751} {:answer/id #uuid "40e107dd-903b-532c-a936-fd8a0507a6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371752} {:answer/id #uuid "62840a4a-671f-55d2-ae1f-8ee90cadeddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+e\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371753} {:answer/id #uuid "935eb2f0-2841-5ad9-bc69-e37faff9662c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+e\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371754} {:answer/id #uuid "6a087499-3ac4-5430-b783-46e925a0bb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "421dd9cb-d37a-51b2-bf54-7fecaeaa111e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+e\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371751} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f4d423-1557-5b46-a95f-3cf4f20875a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371750}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e0286fb-4c1d-5cce-a9a8-0a29399f515e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131119" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{y}+z{e}^{x})\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+{e}^{z})\\,j+(y{e}^{z}+{e}^{x})\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,1) & =({e}^{0}+(1){e}^{0})i+((0){e}^{0}+{e}^{1})j+((0){e}^{1}+{e}^{0})k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+e\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed39b029-85c3-5e97-8954-e0c143d2660e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383225} {:answer/id #uuid "09fedb26-ce0e-5168-aa47-838277d2a8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383226} {:answer/id #uuid "f08ae5ee-40c5-51a6-9971-2dada660033f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383227} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4bca3e-62b0-506e-ab11-64664053f91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383228} {:answer/id #uuid "ec84b4f7-88ba-52da-a5bb-02c8fc23e908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec84b4f7-88ba-52da-a5bb-02c8fc23e908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383229} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2c4aa2-5988-5483-be94-986ea095a0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383224}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14180128-1a3b-5df5-945e-e4a1d12a6375" :question/math-academy-id "q-131117" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}yz-y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}z-x{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+({x}^{3}y-2xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,1) & =(3(1)^{2}(-2)(1)-(-2)(1)^{2})\\,i+((1)^{3}(1)-(1)(1)^{2})\\,j+((1)^{3}(-2)-2(1)(-2)(1))\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-4\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed1a1ab0-6d18-5f30-b80b-be9f46dd80b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398363} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cbc8d4-c2ee-5478-860e-c731a5274aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398364} {:answer/id #uuid "f499f957-93d0-5ca6-8e65-57c931e33b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398365} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aa6514-a3ad-578c-8808-8284836b7b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398366} {:answer/id #uuid "431e58b2-3a4b-54f5-a2fc-05d378bf57ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f499f957-93d0-5ca6-8e65-57c931e33b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398365} :answer-field/id #uuid "530f90fa-b927-569c-b4dd-79831f2442af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398362}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da3be12b-b270-5258-9b1c-409842422d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-131116" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc0eea45-e863-58d6-862a-727382209bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398949} {:answer/id #uuid "f143a8ac-50ee-51ef-b268-61d02a927238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398950} {:answer/id #uuid "997b1e10-3a89-5f14-bd4d-db6ba1a97e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398951} {:answer/id #uuid "da9bac7a-0578-5611-970c-aaa5ef3c56ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398952} {:answer/id #uuid "b37aff02-8b26-5092-a1c6-e4bfb88a16b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b37aff02-8b26-5092-a1c6-e4bfb88a16b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398953} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b666e0-c6c9-5c9c-b88e-9912d89e0283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398948}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0863b6f-83a0-5fff-bd6b-d5b930ec7673" :question/math-academy-id "q-71396" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4643060a-8d1d-5789-a362-102d70382db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} {:answer/id #uuid "8070bb66-d16c-505c-85ee-2a417bc81d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398957} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdcaf70-4358-5973-a594-026557c83288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398958} {:answer/id #uuid "61e68fe8-0332-53d0-b214-d38a39b52899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398959} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c9c882-083f-5978-8947-be71bba2f10e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4643060a-8d1d-5789-a362-102d70382db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} :answer-field/id #uuid "91095769-6695-5735-bdf7-d8d4bfdca38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398955}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58a67c4a-aa8b-5b64-8268-56928e2716de" :question/math-academy-id "q-71399" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd5310c-dfc2-5a7d-bac5-5825f5064da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406775} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd3692c-18d7-5dde-9e79-ca2c71d664a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406776} {:answer/id #uuid "cd07008c-2d5e-5372-aa7f-53d2714271f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406778} {:answer/id #uuid "e415ebda-6949-5999-a629-48685dbda1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406781} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a7f1a1-3119-55e9-ab80-51ececd277a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bd3692c-18d7-5dde-9e79-ca2c71d664a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406776} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f78fc66-8357-5b97-ab1a-5d79ee0e7c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406784}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "077d2dca-c9d8-5043-92cb-18878e5ddc66" :question/math-academy-id "q-71397" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-2,-3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3,1) & =(4(-2)-(-3))\\,i-(-2)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850 :topic/math-academy-id 1799 :topic/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485 :topic/math-academy-id 1376 :topic/title "The Vector Equation of a Line"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e70550-117c-5406-b1d0-1972e33a49a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321186} {:answer/id #uuid "2878fe71-6d72-558f-873e-69c5871866b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321464} {:answer/id #uuid "586da6b9-862c-580b-8d45-8410265668b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321841} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbac82-4e87-5e31-9d1f-ff934afb295a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321999} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7b79a-bbdb-5b22-bcb8-45d937f420aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586da6b9-862c-580b-8d45-8410265668b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321841} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0fe214-7371-518b-8883-59f70ef20780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "967a9051-3e85-5612-827f-8db0c0985ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36343" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ at the point $(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}\\text{ }i-2y\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =\\frac{(2)}{2}\\,i-2(0)\\text{ }j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =i-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9455e98a-4713-54c6-af68-bccd092a2ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{y + 1}{2} = \\frac{z - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123045} {:answer/id #uuid "21291d24-effb-516e-945a-51a6535c2133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = y + 1 = z - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123046} {:answer/id #uuid "5095f082-079e-5ce9-b5ca-ce5407b52ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{y + 1}{2} = z - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123047} {:answer/id #uuid "6f85db0b-f965-5a7a-8ae9-897fe1b293ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2} = \\frac{y + 1}{2} = z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123048} {:answer/id #uuid "768be4cc-7f24-540b-b0f8-718016dd08a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-y - 1 = \\frac{z - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123049}] :answer-field/id #uuid "322e8e14-4251-59c0-abc0-0a9f1a6ccd3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123044} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccafabf2-27cf-5b44-8930-a54e452bc954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-y-1=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385385} {:answer/id #uuid "67a0969d-2efa-50ec-a3a4-619fb8bb16fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385386} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6a97a3-5af6-531a-870a-ae08853e320f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385387} {:answer/id #uuid "7632f3d8-1e29-5bdf-93fe-9967dfc64633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385388} {:answer/id #uuid "247f5172-7db7-511c-8fd9-7db9f6287753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67a0969d-2efa-50ec-a3a4-619fb8bb16fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385386} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6b56a1-3889-545f-a00d-30961c2c2ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385384}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78adb807-d26f-59bb-a8f7-9d0e5cc88b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-37515" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)k \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,-1,1) & =(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\text{ }i+[(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+2(-1)]\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i-2\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{-1}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4345e16b-0df8-59b8-ad37-650db4ac210e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,3,-5⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263593} {:answer/id #uuid "58520017-6d75-5711-9c4c-279ff100c302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨-2,5,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263761} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8b5e1d-96ce-502e-ac9c-14b8cdee3daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264220} {:answer/id #uuid "a89fe459-90af-559f-b732-4d06764bcb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} {:answer/id #uuid "e315f8f8-9238-56c6-b08f-3a589b1dff97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨4,5,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a89fe459-90af-559f-b732-4d06764bcb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90461b1-1149-54d3-9d43-9942adac1495" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265600}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b9d3e0c-64e0-51a4-97be-e5302f43c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131126" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x=5$ at the point $(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fcf330d-a9bc-5cf2-b434-a4434b6c918f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-1,1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263401} {:answer/id #uuid "402f1ba3-23be-5592-8f2a-ad27c9a309bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263569} {:answer/id #uuid "49bbfeb3-58d2-5e76-8091-57f95d4f4d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263633} {:answer/id #uuid "5b15af4c-f9dd-5de5-aecf-f59227dc3406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263785} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dfc3ea-bfe3-57da-b45d-efee09d91a84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,1,-1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "402f1ba3-23be-5592-8f2a-ad27c9a309bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263569} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a93c22f-f072-55e4-a7e9-f76359ecbdf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265404}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23ceddb8-6aa1-5bf0-9ebf-c92f08b6f440" :question/math-academy-id "q-131127" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ at the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f2935d-4872-5cd6-8e24-e225510a7567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=y+1=1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263034} {:answer/id #uuid "18adff3c-3a0d-5f5a-815a-c37ea3624b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x=y+1=1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263235} {:answer/id #uuid "36aacf8e-1fcb-56d4-af5e-18ee04eb5d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263484} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbdaf92-bed5-5318-8e56-f73024adfe3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263790} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6dd442-aa88-5bce-a949-069d0d4b49f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18adff3c-3a0d-5f5a-815a-c37ea3624b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x=y+1=1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263235} :answer-field/id #uuid "db9794d6-ba5f-5c97-afad-c0cc73023f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265631}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec2f8828-cf4a-59b7-bf21-9952b961e912" :question/math-academy-id "q-131128" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}yz=-4$ at the point $(2,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)k \\\\  & =2xyz\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}z\\text{ }j+{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,1) & =2(2)(-1)(1)\\text{ }i+(2)^{2}(1)\\text{ }j+(2)^{2}(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-4)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-4)} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ 2-x=y+1=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00dfea87-0564-54b8-b2ba-b85b9b81f898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=2-y=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263009} {:answer/id #uuid "02bdc94f-aecb-598c-8c8e-fd84d09e16d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=y-2=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263024} {:answer/id #uuid "2143561b-a016-53c4-935f-4521d8cb87c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{y-2}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263298} {:answer/id #uuid "71e1bc5e-929a-5149-b69e-6390e2a09556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263985} {:answer/id #uuid "cd91307b-95b0-5c36-965f-60e3ce8f9695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd91307b-95b0-5c36-965f-60e3ce8f9695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264798} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8f2342-92e1-5c88-9098-f4db5aaee8bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265357}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46c7ac33-5b38-5319-85fa-4f19aa20d07b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131130" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $x={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(3,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013cdae6-0c8a-5577-8cd4-30685db895d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336043} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4309e0-a7c1-56bf-b10e-a0aad8914c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336451} {:answer/id #uuid "4d059734-4e63-596b-82e0-ae11c512dc22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336749} {:answer/id #uuid "bc72b63f-4d93-5d70-823f-f701135f625c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=z-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337706} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9ffc45-7093-5fd9-87c8-ae8b17f9efee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=z+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d059734-4e63-596b-82e0-ae11c512dc22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336749} :answer-field/id #uuid "7690b5ea-01a5-5497-9080-1cc6400cb01b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338481}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5b6f2dff-79f7-5470-a733-d919ff7c2a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-71411" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $3{x}^{2}-xz=6-3xyz$ at the point $(1,0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-xz & =6-3xyz \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)k \\\\  & =(6x-z+3yz)\\text{ }i+3xz\\text{ }j+(-x+3xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,-3) & =(6(1)-(-3)+3(0)(-3))\\text{ }i+3(1)(-3)\\text{ }j+(-1+3(1)(0))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =9\\,i-9\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨9,-9,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y-0}{-9}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dd151b9-79f9-5a66-8557-365b16a9535a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373008} {:answer/id #uuid "e407b4cd-b0e3-5ef2-a3e1-1c2c4a208e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{y}{2}=-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373009} {:answer/id #uuid "2042c239-c608-579c-a356-780f349dc015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{2}=y+1=-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373010} {:answer/id #uuid "e11362e5-c68a-5d93-8627-d0fe7c1b9816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373011} {:answer/id #uuid "dad753bd-efc6-5cc5-bf02-04d7fe01e2cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2042c239-c608-579c-a356-780f349dc015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{2}=y+1=-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373010} :answer-field/id #uuid "f074ec94-f4ab-59e4-98e4-6833b7d43d47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373007}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1e0fe3a-1bd3-5ee4-ab1a-74e46b29c2c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36351" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)$ at the point $(1,-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z$, then our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,0).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2x+y}\\text{ }j-1\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+(-1)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2(1)+(-1)}\\text{ }j-k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2}=y+1=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd12d1d6-f236-57c3-bd6d-b0a476bef5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373141} {:answer/id #uuid "e0818182-cafe-552e-a4a0-98b0c1314881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=\\frac{z+1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373142} {:answer/id #uuid "df5c957f-327c-59b5-a275-854f61c6c1b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y-1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373143} {:answer/id #uuid "4643467a-d570-58d2-ac66-45f1f209b1ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373144} {:answer/id #uuid "c777726a-c485-5a89-bead-338bff4761cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c777726a-c485-5a89-bead-338bff4761cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373145} :answer-field/id #uuid "31f112b8-6ada-53ff-b6b3-be00d57b76b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d41ffa1d-b743-559f-b433-9851a75bb790" :question/math-academy-id "q-71408" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ at the point $(-1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1,1) & =2(-1)\\text{ }i+2(-1)\\text{ }j+2(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-2\\,i-2\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026c9515-f47f-5873-a2ad-edb3df11eb6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383232} {:answer/id #uuid "701877fc-bf3b-56db-a45f-7c6a12a05e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383233} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dc8491-4f00-53bb-a2f3-1cc30657fc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨4,-1,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383234} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb7af74-9d1c-58ad-9346-e5346aec63d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,8⟩+t\\,⟨-1,3,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383235} {:answer/id #uuid "bf422347-5e08-54dc-812b-c19e297a836d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,8⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026c9515-f47f-5873-a2ad-edb3df11eb6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383232} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5bb21b4-51b2-5e7e-bdf4-252b406432c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383231}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fb8c052-74b8-5a3f-8748-9c302788bd84" :question/math-academy-id "q-131124" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}={x}^{2}-6x+12$ at the point $(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=12.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)k \\\\  & =(6-2x)\\,i-8y\\,j+8z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =(6-2(2))\\,i-8(0)\\text{ }j+8(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+8\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "356b8022-b7d2-533d-94f4-726922eda15a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383239} {:answer/id #uuid "e09b8534-738c-5763-a9b2-970a08b191e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=y+1=\\frac{z+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383240} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55da50-663f-5be8-a53f-b873d7c761d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=y+1=\\frac{z+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383241} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5e3e97-6073-5e4d-bc6c-d357a63433cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=z+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383242} {:answer/id #uuid "ec59022a-f4b9-5231-92ea-f9fef2e55eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x=y+1=z+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "356b8022-b7d2-533d-94f4-726922eda15a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383239} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee6c88e-5b3b-502a-94c3-437af9351333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383238}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d47e612d-20bf-521c-bc95-454c7d00a756" :question/math-academy-id "q-131129" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz=-3$ at the point $(2,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-3.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}-2yz)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}-2xz)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}-2xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,-1) & =({e}^{2-1-1}-2(-1)(-1))\\text{ }i+({e}^{2-1-1}-2(2)(-1))\\text{ }j+({e}^{2-1-1}-2(2)(-1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =({e}^{0}-2)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0}+4)\\text{ }j+({e}^{0}+4)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i+5\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{5}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5} \\\\ 2-x=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a64d29b1-e2ba-5e48-b58f-cbe663d22375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385379} {:answer/id #uuid "12178fb2-90d2-55f5-8547-92f9528d658e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385380} {:answer/id #uuid "81c5f623-6c5c-5ccc-9c48-e4f0e0bb30c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{z-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385381} {:answer/id #uuid "80aa2bbc-5891-5ead-81a6-a88d99d82419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385382} {:answer/id #uuid "c7afb82c-1d04-5f3a-b6b5-2604eb2833f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=y-1=\\frac{2-z}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12178fb2-90d2-55f5-8547-92f9528d658e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385380} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b8dbb5c-29ef-5a51-b4a1-be1ea897551a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "780bb1b8-cd5e-553c-8c69-1da3293bbe1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37232" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}=1$ at the point $(2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$     \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1,2).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1,2):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{z-2}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e84be6-fb21-5888-bde0-2b1fc8861800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403470} {:answer/id #uuid "125b86ba-8a17-5cc7-bb9b-688c88040c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403471} {:answer/id #uuid "a563a551-487d-52b0-b121-65d93d53f35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403472} {:answer/id #uuid "74e61dbd-b0b0-51fb-aee1-a797c08c8fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403473} {:answer/id #uuid "65d0bd41-c0f7-5813-a9d5-53b92a3b0d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65d0bd41-c0f7-5813-a9d5-53b92a3b0d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a86f3ea-971f-5c0c-ab0d-7a86359ca0e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403469}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca7dad4d-8007-5c21-9019-38d66b5366c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71407" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4(\\frac{1}{2})-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124556c0-f467-5318-900e-7da585d6554e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405134} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a1b1e-b535-5117-9c36-e366c14a4377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405135} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1353fa-aa2b-5093-bcea-f8bdbde39ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405136} {:answer/id #uuid "699346b8-439a-5fe0-9c40-fa77c142405e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405137} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce9fec0-c766-50ce-8550-9639ade8ba3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a1b1e-b535-5117-9c36-e366c14a4377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0a835b-df77-526f-b20f-c5410f491aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405133}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7c74ce4-3c4e-5ea4-ab88-89558065f279" :question/math-academy-id "q-36754" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ at the point $(0,0,2),$ with parameter $t=0$ at this point." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954 :topic/math-academy-id 177 :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Components"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999 :topic/math-academy-id 4139 :topic/title "Differentiating Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/math-academy-id 3173 :topic/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d91284b-aa86-5efd-8e00-bc13b952dcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321627} {:answer/id #uuid "57674ee3-9965-5502-a818-bbcc31f0235a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321835} {:answer/id #uuid "60879608-43b6-5c2d-8f50-f7877f002d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321904} {:answer/id #uuid "8305ad0c-8f86-50aa-a55a-367d449dabc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322180} {:answer/id #uuid "e18c50bc-afa0-58da-b693-f0e3601201ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60879608-43b6-5c2d-8f50-f7877f002d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321904} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71b9994-814b-5c24-af5b-f67cb4ffae0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323654}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ea55a51-bc91-526a-9277-7689869bc683" :question/math-academy-id "q-36211" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y={e}^{-t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a652f4fa-5e51-550b-ae0f-acf975b5ad30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t + 1}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124095} {:answer/id #uuid "c951b256-32ff-5f68-b860-7cb5bb3678fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 - t\\tan t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124096} {:answer/id #uuid "d94d7c62-b302-5dfb-b7aa-0f1dc5f7e8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^{2}\\tan t + 2}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124097} {:answer/id #uuid "26161a3b-9d9f-5127-866f-e9b489af75b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 - t\\sec t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124098} {:answer/id #uuid "2e51b451-2a42-5fa4-9919-4c8fb375df52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^{2}\\sec t + 1}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124099}] :answer-field/id #uuid "00f4d712-e22f-5cf5-b66e-67d08872e484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124094}] :question/id #uuid "72c45146-95bd-53af-b2f0-49b5ef23b12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95056" :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = \\ln xy$ and $r(t) = \\cos ti + t^{2}j$, compute $\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))]$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1ec52e-ccdc-5b0f-b240-92720661c62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+9{t}^{3}+6{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321508} {:answer/id #uuid "5c35907b-6462-5098-94af-12ec14663f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-3t-3t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321877} {:answer/id #uuid "82fd3b74-e5d5-5eb9-84d2-cb77ba0c33b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-3{t}^{2}+36{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322179} {:answer/id #uuid "ac96cf45-01f8-5f94-b48d-b88b9ae7af6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{2}-12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322499} {:answer/id #uuid "d89116e6-d4ee-5b61-abf5-08714b411c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d89116e6-d4ee-5b61-abf5-08714b411c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842} :answer-field/id #uuid "d379d752-f7ce-576e-9786-94b4166ca354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323585}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1916a81-682a-57cf-b7a5-c43c18c456ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-37099" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2x-x{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2-{y}^{2})\\,i-2xy\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=3{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0661fd29-a292-5ab4-b31a-2396be427221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357808} {:answer/id #uuid "2c19e560-7929-538c-bcc2-9884cff52e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} {:answer/id #uuid "bb354d15-c911-57ac-961b-2bf623e8c47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359378} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd453d2-7215-5f3b-8c26-6cee0b42f4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359383} {:answer/id #uuid "caaabb05-4b0e-56ee-9471-80ef684ce597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c19e560-7929-538c-bcc2-9884cff52e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c5c1098-6416-500c-946c-2648c90f4466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360078}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eac0cd5-9d30-54ff-89b8-2cfd1b9003d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95052" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)=\\pi t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\pi t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j)\\cdot (\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\pi {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e56dc23-76eb-5db9-9fce-e4d36ef6a300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3({t}^{2}+1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358426} {:answer/id #uuid "50d39335-f934-5320-9bd6-5f69635a09d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}{e}^{t}+2}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358445} {:answer/id #uuid "7dab4a7a-ef04-5286-8145-c994f7fd0f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} {:answer/id #uuid "de80fcbe-829a-5dae-a81d-fb3cf700a555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359706} {:answer/id #uuid "eb612ba8-e57f-520d-9b63-e9ab14d4e478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t{e}^{t}-1)}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dab4a7a-ef04-5286-8145-c994f7fd0f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed81ec3-d906-5a86-bc7c-31b1ba6834fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99c33c9d-4cdd-5e75-a91b-a13201898eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-95054" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={t}^{-2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{2}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{-2}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{-2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{e}^{t}}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{-2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-2({t}^{2})({t}^{-3})+2{e}^{-t}({e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f3235b-60e2-5c23-a6d1-2912bfc76c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358041} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fdac84-5c94-5bcb-847f-c1a99a8c5411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359347} {:answer/id #uuid "bde855a4-7fc2-52c3-af6f-b2e1fd0d6c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359405} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8b8503-66f4-52dd-b74c-bee16b502323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359689} {:answer/id #uuid "e953648a-9a4f-5892-9fbd-2c82540d2fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde855a4-7fc2-52c3-af6f-b2e1fd0d6c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359405} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3efa02-f1ae-5df0-98fd-0df55d2c7698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360170}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d03333b-22a3-579a-b479-87085056a6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95059" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}]}
